            <trans-unit id="FXZbjDI">
                <source>description.seo_humans_from</source>
                <target>Country the site is run from, as shown in humans.txt, e.g. France</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="DA0nXQ4">
                <source>description.seo_humans_thanks</source>
                <target>The "THANKS" block of humans.txt, one credit per line (frameworks, authors, contributors).</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="aqkZyv.">
                <source>description.seo_llms_summary</source>
                <target>One-sentence summary of the site, used as the quoted introduction of llms.txt. Left empty, and unless a bundle declares titled urls, no llms.txt is written at all.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="IHEwehJ">
                <source>description.seo_robots_ai_crawlers</source>
                <target>The crawlers blocked when the setting above is on, as a JSON array. Answer engines that cite the pages they read (Claude-User, OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot...) are deliberately absent: blocking them only costs visibility.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="EUyn9gj">
                <source>description.seo_robots_ai_crawlers_source</source>
                <target>The community list the monthly health check compares yours to, and the "Update the AI crawlers" shortcut adds from. Set it to "none" to keep your list by hand: nothing is fetched then. Left empty, the entry is written back with the default source at the next config load.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="lIc9kKR">
                <source>description.seo_robots_block_ai</source>
                <target>Blocks the crawlers harvesting pages to train models. On by default. Answer engines (Claude-User, OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot…) are never blocked: they read a page to answer a question and cite it back, and they are what reads the llms.txt. Advisory in any case, nothing enforces it.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="cJxfsLp">
                <source>description.seo_robots_disallow</source>
                <target>Paths robots.txt forbids, as a JSON array (e.g. ["/*.pdf$"]). Empty, the whole site is crawlable.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="PelrJJG">
                <source>description.seo_robots_extra</source>
                <target>Written as typed inside the "User-agent: *" group of robots.txt, for whatever the settings above don't cover. A private site declares nothing besides its own rule, so these lines are left out of it.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="dhRax_b">
                <source>description.seo_robots_private</source>
                <target>Keeps this site out of search engines: robots.txt then forbids all crawling, and no llms.txt is written. Advisory only - what must not be reached has to sit behind authentication, never behind this setting.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="MlHHZXH">
                <source>description.site_address</source>
                <target>The seller's postal address, used by the withdrawal form of the terms of sales. Optional, leave it empty for a site run from a home.</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="ZDgy5oO">
                <source>description.site_author</source>
                <target>The author that will be set in the meta data</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="PZrDv2T">
                <source>description.site_backup_database</source>
                <target>Name of the MySQL database to back up</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="zquqL77">
                <source>description.site_backup_db_host</source>
                <target>MySQL host for backups</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="y563wVd">
                <source>description.site_backup_db_password</source>
                <target>MySQL password for backups</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="P7VAMy1">
                <source>description.site_backup_db_user</source>
                <target>MySQL user for backups</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="3_4rjYZ">
                <source>description.site_backup_mailto</source>
                <target>Email address for backup reports and errors</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="XmN1LNH">
                <source>description.site_backup_max_age_hours</source>
                <target>Past this long without a recorded backup, the dashboard raises an alert. It is the only signal a backup that stopped running altogether can produce</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="UO9CA09">
                <source>description.site_backup_offsite_keep_days</source>
                <target>How many days the offsite copy keeps overwritten or deleted files (deleted/ folders). 0 purges nothing, which is what destinations taking their own snapshots already handle</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="L8.ybZ0">
                <source>description.site_backup_offsite_max_age_hours</source>
                <target>Past this long without a copy leaving the server, the dashboard alerts. A backup that never left the machine protects nothing</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="l7P49Wf">
                <source>description.site_backup_offsite_target</source>
                <target>rclone destination of the offsite copy, as "remote:path" (eg. storagebox:975l.com). Credentials stay in rclone's own configuration, never here. Left empty, nothing is sent: that's the mode where an outside machine comes and pulls the backups</target>
            </trans-unit>
            <trans-unit id="OV1T_sI">
                <source>description.site_backup_retention_days</source>
                <target>Number of days of backups kept on the server. Whoever copies them offsite must keep more than this, otherwise the next copy downloads again what it has just purged. 0 keeps every archive</target>
            </trans-unit>
